TITLE:: FxPresetStore
summary:: named settings - the ones the library ships and yours
categories:: Libraries>ParaEQ
related:: Classes/ParaEQ, Classes/Compressor, Classes/FxUnit

DESCRIPTION::
Every effect class keeps one of these: a list of the presets it was written
with, and after them the ones you have saved, which live in a file of your own
between sessions. The store does not care what a preset holds - an array of eq
bands, an Event of compressor settings - only that it can be written with
code::asCompileString:: and read back with code::load::.

code::
ParaEQ.presetStore.names;
ParaEQ.presetStore.at(\warm);
ParaEQ.presetStore.add("my room", ParaEQ.defaultBands);
ParaEQ.presetStore.remove("my room");
::

A name the library ships is refused rather than shadowed: those are what every
saved kit and chain is written against. Adding, removing and clearing announce
code::\presets:: on the owning class, so every open window hears about it.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: owner
The class that announces code::\presets::.
ARGUMENT:: builtins
code::name, data, name, data...:: in menu order.
ARGUMENT:: file
Where your presets are kept.
ARGUMENT:: legacyFile
Read once if code::file:: does not exist yet, and written under the new name.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: all
METHOD:: names
The built in ones first, then yours. Reading them is what brings yours in from
disk.

METHOD:: at
A copy of one preset's data, or nil.

METHOD:: includes
METHOD:: isBuiltin
METHOD:: isUser

METHOD:: detect
The name some settings are, or nil once you have moved something.

METHOD:: add
Keep some settings under a name. Writing over one of yours is fine; a built in
name is refused. Answers the name, or nil.

METHOD:: remove
Drop one of yours.

METHOD:: clear
Drop all of yours, on disk as well.

METHOD:: file
METHOD:: legacyFile
METHOD:: owner
METHOD:: builtins
METHOD:: userPresets
